      "topic": {
        "text": "control flow statements",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null,
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "Control flow statements include conditional statements such as if and switch for making decisions, and loops such as for, while, do‑while, and foreach for executing code repeatedly. Break and continue statements can alter loop execution.",
        "refs": ["Q0001", "Q0002"]
      }
    },
    {
      "topic": {
        "text": "code maintainability",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "explanation_type": null,
      "explanation_text": {
        "text": "When a method becomes too long, it hurts maintainability because readers must scan many statements to understand the logic. Breaking the code into smaller, well‑named chunks improves readability and maintainability.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      }
    }
  ],
  "examples": [],
  "facts": [
    {
      "fact_type": null,
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Dividing code into smaller, logical sections makes it easier to read and understand.",
        "refs": ["Q0000", "Q0001"]
      }
    },
    {
      "fact_type": null,
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Good programmers write code that humans can understand, not just code that a computer can execute.",
